{"step_index":1505,"source":"MODEL","type":"GENERIC","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-11-12T01:52:35Z","content":"Created At: 2026-11-12T09:52:35+08:00\nCompleted At: 2026-11-12T09:52:44+08:00\nCreated At: 2026-11-12T09:52:35+08:00\nCompleted At: 2026-11-12T09:52:44+08:00\nThe search for \"SQLite WAL replace database file while open in another process corruption backup API\" returned the following summary:\nReplacing or overwriting an SQLite database file on disk while it is actively open and in use by another process—especially when **WAL (Write-Ahead Logging) mode** is enabled—is a direct recipe for **database corruption or sudden application crashes**[1][2]. \n\n---\n\n### Why File-Level Replacement Fails in WAL Mode\n\n1. **Multiple Files Constitute the Database:** In WAL mode, SQLite relies on three files working in unison: `database.db` (the main file), `database.db-wal` (the write-ahead log containing recent committed transactions), and `database.db-shm` (shared memory index)[3][4]. \n2. **Stale/Mismatched WAL State:** If you overwrite only the main `database.db` file with a new version while leaving the old process running, the target process still holds an open file descriptor/handle to the *old* content, or it will look at the existing `-wal` and `-shm` files which do not match the new database pages[2][5]. This mismatch causes immediate logical corruption (`SQLITE_CORRUPT`)[6].\n3. **OS-Level Locking Behavioral Differences:** \n   * On **Windows**, you cannot overwrite an open file at all; the file replacement operation will throw a \"file in use / permission denied\" error.\n   * On **Linux/Unix**, you *can* delete or overwrite a file inode while a process has it open, but the running process keeps writing/reading the *old* unlinked file descriptor, meaning it will completely ignore your replacement file until restarted[2].\n\n---\n\n### The Correct Solutions\n\nDepending on your workflow, you should use either the **SQLite Online Backup API** or coordinate a **clean application-level restart**.\n\n#### Solution 1: Use the SQLite Online Backup API (Programmatic Replacement)\nIf you want to update/replace the contents of a live database \n<truncated 2094 bytes>\n/AUZIYQEjLZ-2BDDUyTiQKP8aaJM6skddjK91gK_zxswX8T1JzO1vCFb6LxZfS8OejiQ_HlRIZ1onygoCGMqkw_SmAqbve2ohvPlWO8JTwmmQbzgxnkr7dJnpmAj2dEZS2TOHtU8EYQQB3vpQK0uBW8TVR3d7QHTfZO8IZK7UyLmZMTfoLxfub8upjlsB8t7RhjpOUluqoGg=)\n[2] [stackoverflow.com](https://vertexaisearch.cloud.google.com/grounding-api-redirect/AUZIYQH2e2pdMceiOE4m23kQIvvNWKSwVsSXYxVGaPdq7gKz5LV80CAvN9FlgCPIaGcV3y2ymATjUwPMfNcSSN0Kqp1xdHTTBsoSg2ljrehLTX_MAKcOSrRboZlw0b87zl-FrkswyT9Pq7A_m7BX5Zx7_QFT8zZRspOrlZPPRbbC5B_ss7XgdWWkhqa5VptV5ac=)\n[3] [dev.to](https://vertexaisearch.cloud.google.com/grounding-api-redirect/AUZIYQE4FPtazQW8whleTcuhDSQ3YoakdhLUge7YMwxOiex4dVhoXk_s4_NbMgYZg63jcimm0zq78f4NAeGInmYOJE2TmDwo3bTgOJgzrlaEgJfLjf7EmafTKQ8PVj9wHnwLws6L4DtJVfubnmBiGfgq-fvE8kuxjvcpAvEdnFKJX7I-0Z8wsKulZw-p6NYXwsL4H02umV4vce-rJBufsowM7Sa0H6WPpA==)\n[4] [coddy.tech](https://vertexaisearch.cloud.google.com/grounding-api-redirect/AUZIYQGyVc8aUwPs9TYdBNUUeV5wfqT9hMwx35lG0TDh5NzKNS-ksDe33gJpf8m1PEaIfeldtMsqQCR1v1mWKJvqV9kRw2xYlfKx_JwhGWTFbjlHAlghYBHPoZLKqyHgIG1wJTBfRSPEDaTNGsDezkw=)\n[5] [sqlite.org](https://vertexaisearch.cloud.google.com/grounding-api-redirect/AUZIYQE6HxbPcpwsXQlruBm5T5-sNqOd-wqfgllwSZVT3Z_GnAMhhiZwdHhu4W8E9B4a45zV0kNOrmFQLJGOKOvd0EqB3OlWOacpNa2ntCL9JuUdzFMKFH91DmZZltZCg_GAbfzWnPg=)\n[6] [scottspence.com](https://vertexaisearch.cloud.google.com/grounding-api-redirect/AUZIYQHOigwLdgPSDDSSIJd-wYiXOcJNv_JNg0I8i1qZxlu6dAmAAXH55yHNNeK3fpt8aAoZt14ROpl_dOJdjOa6DN8wE-nWYTg12vy-caIlI8hIPMpmL4Cz0zpfBKR5su3pT-WtlY9wmvJcBf5LVcxXwb8U8NVrFyRq)\n[7] [kunalganglani.com](https://vertexaisearch.cloud.google.com/grounding-api-redirect/AUZIYQE0qtT84jr85PSdPjBUgJynHhiu_Y0YvZLikpqQIQemsl0N5pc9_G2uIv6s-IB8iRfR8M4KlVnLbVRf5B8pJBG-_x1-i9jYr60IZgorAiQTEY2_4i0awnUiysOpKEVSILjVpDyUiW-JNxRC6pPIzB1sRw==)\n[8] [sqlite.org](https://